yeah, that makes sense.

34
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,960
All right, so just starting with the basics.

35
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:21,980
So how many of you have ever heard

36
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:23,780
of the median voter theorem at some point?

37
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:26,840
All right, good, we'll just do a very quick version of it.

38
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:28,980
All right, so first of all,

39
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:32,020
let us assume that voters' preferences are what we call

40
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:35,480
single-peaked, single-peaked preferences.

41
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:39,420
This means everybody has an ideal point or a bliss point,

42
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:42,680
and utility declines monotonically

43
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:44,460
as policy moves away from it.

44
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:46,960
This would mean, for example,

45
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:50,280
if you're going to have a bliss temperature of 74 degrees,

46
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:53,440
and then your utility goes down automatically,

47
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:56,240
the further away you get, the lower your utility becomes.

48
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:59,360
All right, well, wouldn't everything be like that?

49
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:00,560
No, not necessarily.

50
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:03,040
So, for example, suppose that you like skiing,

51
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:04,080
but you also like swimming.

52
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:07,880
In that case, you might have double-peaked preferences,

53
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:13,540
where you might have one peak at, say, 33 degrees, 32 degrees,

54
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:15,640
say, so that is the warmest temperature

55
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:16,540
at which you can have snow.

56
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:20,040
And then as it gets colder, that's where snow is.

57
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:21,820
So, that's the worst for you, because then you're snowing

58
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,100
under less pleasant conditions.

59
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:25,840
But as it gets warmer, that's also less pleasant,

60
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:27,640
because then your probability of having snow

61
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:28,960
or the duration of snow goes down.

62
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:31,160
And then that could go down for a while,

63
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:32,600
and then it could go up again,

64
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,060
and then you might have 74 degrees, the speed drops more,

65
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:36,800
and then it goes down again.

66
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:40,280
So, that would be a case where we have two peaks, right?

67
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:44,940
Okay? Or to take a political example, Lenin,

68
00:02:44,940 --> 00:02:47,520
your Lenin had a slogan, the worse the better,

69
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:48,960
the worse the better.

70
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:52,540
By which he meant that he actually wanted worse things

71
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:54,880
to happen, so that there could be,

72
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:56,420
that people would be extremely unhappy,

73
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:57,520
and then there could be a revolution,

74
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:59,380
and he could establish a communist dictatorship,

75
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,780
rather than having an intermediate option.

76
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:05,060
So, he preferred the rule of the czar, who was much further away

77
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:08,160
from him, than the slightly more modern Marxists,

78
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:09,880
who he considered the very worst,

79
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:12,880
because they were the ones that were going to put forward

80
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:15,600
reformist measures, which would prevent the possibility

81
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,020
of the really good thing, a dictatorship run by Lenin.

82
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:23,000
Or one other example, during the Vietnam War,

83
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,060
one view commonly expressed was, we shouldn't be there at all,

84
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:28,440
but if we're there, we should be there to win.

85
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:34,000
Right? So, either do nothing, or go in with your nuclear weapons,

86
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:35,760
but don't do the intermediate thing.

87
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:37,640
That again, so it would be something

88
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:39,560
like two peaks in the preferences.

89
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:40,900
All right.

90
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:43,000
Now, suppose we've got a two-party

91
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:44,320
or a two-candidate election,

92
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:46,880
and then imagine the voters care about,

93
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:49,160
and are perfectly informed about, just one issue,

94
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:51,120
which is liberal versus conservative,

95
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:53,160
or of course, that could be left versus right.

96
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:54,080
All right.

97
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:55,600
And then, assume the winner,

98
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,280
the electoral rule is winner takes all,

99
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:59,320
so whoever gets more votes automatically wins.

100
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:03,800
We will assume that all ties resolve by flipping a coin.

101
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,080
In the real world, of course, elections are almost never ties,

102
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:09,280
and so we don't have to worry about what the tie rule is.

103
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:11,880
There is, in fact, a very detailed tie rule

104
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:13,240
for U.S. presidential elections.

105
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:15,080
A student once emailed it to me.

106
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:16,720
It's seven steps.

107
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:17,200
All right.

108
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:19,280
But what if all seven things are satisfied?

109
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:20,880
Maybe there's some final thing, but anyway.

110
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:24,800
But in theoretical models, ties happen all the time.

111
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:28,960
And so, it is important when you're doing electoral models,

112
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:31,040
to come up with your rule for what happens under ties.

113
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:31,960
All right.

114
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:33,520
Then, we need to make an assumption

115
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:35,840
about candidate motivation, which is they want to win.

116
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:37,360
Right? They want to win.

117
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:39,240
Right? And particularly, they care more

118
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:40,760
about everything else put together.

119
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:44,640
I'm just having a conversation with Steve Pearlstein,

120
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:46,600
where he claimed this was a ridiculous assumption.

121
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:50,240
I was puzzled by why anyone would consider it ridiculous.

122
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:53,160
Just saying, well, just look at history

123
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,400
and the horrible things people have done to gain power.

124
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:59,520
Doesn't that indicate they really want power

125
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:01,920
and will do these things in order to get it?

126
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:04,280
But he assured me if I actually mess

127
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:05,680
with politicians, I should change my mind.

128
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:08,480
I suppose it's possible.

129
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:09,360
It doesn't seem likely.

130
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:10,280
All right.

131
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:12,400
I also said that you can't really believe what people

132
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:16,200
like that say, because they're trying to tell you what you want

133
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:16,560
to hear.

134
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:18,240
And people don't want a politician

135
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:20,640
to say, I seek power.

136
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:21,440
All right.

137
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:22,760
Doesn't mean they don't.

138
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:24,440
Right? It's just not a good thing to say,

139
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:26,080
especially if you're seeking power.

140
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:27,560
You don't say, you want power.

141
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:29,000
You don't say, I seek absolute power.

142
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,680
You say, I just want to save the people here.

143
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:35,800
The last thing I want is power for myself.

144
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:39,120
Anyway. So, we assume that they care about winning more

145
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:40,840
than everything else put together.

146
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:42,800
And then, last assumption is they can compete

147
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:46,040
in exactly one way by taking a stand on the issue.

148
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:46,800
It's not even an issue.

149
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:49,480
It's just only one issue in a simple model.

150
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:50,080
All right.

151
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:52,360
Now, if we go and graph this distribution

152
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:56,120
of the bliss points, this shows there is that density of people

153
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:58,080
who consider that their favorite thing.

154
00:05:58,080 --> 00:05:59,920
And there's that density of people who consider

155
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:01,720
that their favorite thing.

156
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:01,960
All right.

157
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:04,600
Now, notice, since this is only a distribution of belief points

158
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:06,240
or other bliss points,

159
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:10,440
these people might have very different utility functions.

160
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:12,040
So, this, one of the, some people

161
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:15,360
like this might have very symmetric utility functions.

162
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,680
But a person, another, a person could have exactly the same bliss

163
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:22,160
point but could have a very slow fade on this side.

164
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:24,080
And a very, or excuse me, very fast fade on this side

165
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:25,920
and a very slow fade on that side.

166
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:28,880
So, you both have the same maximum, the same first choice.

167
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:32,960
And yet, one person could be indifferent between moves

168
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:34,880
in a liberal or conservative direction.

169
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:37,720
But another person could say, look, this is the right amount

170
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:41,440
of liberalism, which is a lot, but anyone more liberal

171
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,160
than me is crazy.

172
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:44,680
Whereas, you can be a lot less liberal

173
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:47,160
than me and still be an okay person.

174
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:50,120
So, that could be your view as well.

175
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:52,280
Okay. All right.

176
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:53,720
Now, what, oh, yes, yes.

177
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:54,080
Yes.

