211
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:15,860
Now once we realize, so this is our distribution here.

212
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:18,960
Now we can imagine going and putting down

213
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:20,660
just randomly our initial positions.

214
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:22,220
So suppose this is the,

215
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:26,580
what's called the D party, Democratic Party.

216
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:29,980
And this is our Republican Party platform initially.

217
00:07:29,980 --> 00:07:32,380
Now given the assumptions that I just told you,

218
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:35,980
we can divide this diagram into three regions.

219
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:37,740
Region one, these are the people

220
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:40,260
who definitely vote for the Democrats

221
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:42,460
because they are more liberal than the Democrats.

222
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:44,220
And if people have single peak preferences,

223
00:07:44,220 --> 00:07:46,420
you always choose which evil?

224
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:49,980
The lesser evil or greater evil.

225
00:07:49,980 --> 00:07:51,340
Yes, if you have single peak preferences,

226
00:07:51,340 --> 00:07:53,660
you always choose the lesser evil.

227
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:55,380
That's the nature of single peak preferences,

228
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:58,900
is saying which is the lesser evil and I will do that.

229
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:01,620
All right, similarly over here we have region three.

230
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:03,920
These are the people whose bliss point is more conservative

231
00:08:03,920 --> 00:08:05,140
than the conservative opposition,

232
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:08,820
so they will all vote for the conservative candidates.

233
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:11,140
And then we have people in region two.

234
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:12,420
Now it's very tempting to say,

235
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:14,580
oh well all that happens is you split it.

236
00:08:14,580 --> 00:08:17,780
But we don't have enough assumptions to say that.

237
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:20,580
Because remember it's possible that this guy,

238
00:08:20,580 --> 00:08:23,780
that someone here could have a very fast fall

239
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:25,980
in their utility function on the right

240
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:28,220
and a very slow fall on the left,

241
00:08:28,220 --> 00:08:31,260
so a person who's very close to the Republican

242
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:34,500
might still prefer the Democrats.

243
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:35,340
That is possible.

244
00:08:36,340 --> 00:08:37,540
All right, now you may say,

245
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:40,540
oh well this is, not only did you make a bunch of assumptions

246
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:41,540
but you didn't even make enough of them.

247
00:08:41,540 --> 00:08:43,180
We need even more to get an answer.

248
00:08:43,180 --> 00:08:45,940
But it turns out that this model still can be solved

249
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:48,380
with no more assumptions than I already gave you.

250
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:53,060
How can that be done?

251
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:56,620
Well, think about being a paid political advisor

252
00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:58,340
to either side.

253
00:08:58,340 --> 00:08:59,860
And you understand the nature of the model,

254
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:01,460
so the Democrat comes and says,

255
00:09:01,460 --> 00:09:04,300
what do I need to do in order to get more votes?

256
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:06,140
And in the context of this model,

257
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:07,780
what is the right answer going to be?

258
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:08,620
Move to the middle.

259
00:09:08,620 --> 00:09:09,460
Yes, what's that?

260
00:09:09,460 --> 00:09:10,300
Move to the middle.

261
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:12,420
Yes, move to the middle, moderate.

262
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:13,900
Moderate, Democrats.

263
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:15,820
Move over here, because when you do that,

264
00:09:15,820 --> 00:09:18,060
you expand area one, so now you scoop up

265
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:19,820
all those extra votes for sure,

266
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:22,940
because now those people are more liberal than you.

267
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:25,660
And you don't lose a single vote in there

268
00:09:25,660 --> 00:09:28,500
because preferences are single-peat.

269
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:30,900
So at minimum you gain these

270
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:32,580
and you don't lose a single person in there

271
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:34,540
and so you have increased your vote share.

272
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:38,580
Now, since the liberal got more conservative

273
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:40,540
in order to win, does the conservative

274
00:09:40,540 --> 00:09:42,260
also get more conservative to win?

275
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:43,940
No.

276
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:45,940
No, the conservative does what in order to win?

277
00:09:45,940 --> 00:09:46,780
Moderates.

278
00:09:46,780 --> 00:09:47,740
Moderates.

279
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:48,820
Once again, he moderates.

280
00:09:48,820 --> 00:09:50,860
So the advisor to the conservative says,

281
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:52,380
all right, I'm gonna go in there.

282
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:54,940
And then again, you scoop up those votes for sure

283
00:09:54,940 --> 00:09:57,500
and you don't lose a single one in there.

284
00:09:57,500 --> 00:10:00,020
Right, now of course, this is just like

285
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:01,700
the kind of thing where you're in a market

286
00:10:01,700 --> 00:10:05,260
and there's a shortage, so you raise the price a bit

287
00:10:05,260 --> 00:10:06,660
and there's still a shortage,

288
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:08,420
and so you raise the price again.

289
00:10:08,420 --> 00:10:10,820
You raise the price again until something happens.

290
00:10:10,820 --> 00:10:13,380
So it makes sense to tell the Democrat to moderate again

291
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:15,020
and the Republican to moderate again.

292
00:10:15,020 --> 00:10:16,980
And if they always wanna keep moderating,

293
00:10:16,980 --> 00:10:19,140
moving closer to the other side,

294
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:22,820
then the only equilibrium situation is gonna be one

295
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:24,980
where the platform of the Democrat

296
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:27,900
doubles the platform of the Republican.

297
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:30,300
All right, and so in equilibrium,

298
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:31,740
the platforms cannot be different

299
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:34,060
because you can always improve your position

300
00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:36,820
by moving closer to the other candidates.

301
00:10:36,820 --> 00:10:38,460
That's our first equilibrium condition

302
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:40,020
in the median voter model.

303
00:10:41,740 --> 00:10:45,260
Okay, yes, so literally that means that one party

304
00:10:45,260 --> 00:10:46,820
can just copy the platform of the other

305
00:10:46,820 --> 00:10:49,620
and then switch out the worst Democrat or Republican

306
00:10:49,620 --> 00:10:52,140
and that would be the end of it, okay,

307
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:54,140
if you take it very literally.

308
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:56,340
Okay, so, and there's a special name for this.

309
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:59,140
This is called the platform convergence

310
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:02,060
where the platforms converge, they become the same.

311
00:11:02,060 --> 00:11:04,700
Like there's a famous line from I believe George Wallace

312
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:07,060
who said, there's not a dime's worth of difference

313
00:11:07,060 --> 00:11:09,820
between the two parties, right?

314
00:11:09,820 --> 00:11:13,340
Which people usually say to make it sound like

315
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:16,380
there's not competition and democracy.

316
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:20,220
But actually in this model, that is beside the competition.

317
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:21,820
Just like we see that all the wheat farmers

318
00:11:21,820 --> 00:11:25,380
charge the same price, ah, collusion, right?

319
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:30,380
No, the more normal assumption is competition, right?

320
00:11:30,540 --> 00:11:32,980
Because with competition, anyone who charges a higher price

321
00:11:32,980 --> 00:11:35,100
than anybody else loses all their business.

322
00:11:35,100 --> 00:11:36,500
So you don't wanna do that.

323
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:39,180
All right, but saying the platforms converge

324
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:42,340
does not answer the question to what do they converge?

325
00:11:42,340 --> 00:11:44,540
So what do they converge to?

326
00:11:44,540 --> 00:11:47,020
And now I'm going to lead the witness to get to the answer.

327
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:49,300
I didn't invent this myself, of course.

328
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:51,220
Have you ever had a math problem where the teacher said,

329
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:53,900
let's just try a solution of the form

330
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:58,540
Y equals tangent of U, see what happens, right?

331
00:11:58,540 --> 00:12:01,660
And it almost always works.

332
00:12:01,660 --> 00:12:03,300
And of course your teacher didn't come up with it.

333
00:12:03,300 --> 00:12:05,380
You got it from his teacher who also didn't come up with it.

334
00:12:05,380 --> 00:12:08,660
Got it from his teacher, sell it and then gouse.

335
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:09,500
They didn't.

336
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:12,660
All right, so similarly here, I didn't come up with this.

337
00:12:12,660 --> 00:12:13,700
My teacher didn't come up with this.

338
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:16,540
But somewhere, someone came up with it.

339
00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:20,420
I think it was Anthony Downs, it's Duncan Black,

340
00:12:20,420 --> 00:12:21,420
if I remember, right?

341
00:12:22,740 --> 00:12:23,700
Maybe, all right.

342
00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:26,500
Anyway, so first leading question.

343
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:29,140
Is it possible that they could both converge

344
00:12:29,140 --> 00:12:34,060
to a position that is above the median distribution?

345
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:36,860
So say, could they both converge to the 80th percentile?

346
00:12:38,780 --> 00:12:40,860
And the answer of course is no,

347
00:12:40,860 --> 00:12:42,660
because if you're both at the 80th,

348
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:45,780
one party could move to the 79th percentile

349
00:12:45,780 --> 00:12:48,420
and then get a minimum of 79% of the votes

350
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:49,260
and win for sure.

351
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:53,540
Okay, so and of course if you are at the 52nd percentile,

352
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:56,020
one guy just moves to the 51st percentile

353
00:12:56,020 --> 00:12:59,140
and gets a minimum of 51% of the votes for sure

354
00:12:59,140 --> 00:13:00,460
and therefore wins.

355
00:13:00,460 --> 00:13:03,500
So we've got the next equilibrium condition saying

356
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:06,900
that the platforms to which they converge

357
00:13:06,900 --> 00:13:10,260
must be greater than or equal to the bliss point

358
00:13:10,260 --> 00:13:13,020
of the median person in the distribution.

359
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:16,060
All right, now we can go and do another thought experiment.

360
00:13:16,060 --> 00:13:18,820
Say, could they both converge to the 48th percentile?

361
00:13:18,820 --> 00:13:21,420
So they both converge to something below the median.

362
00:13:21,420 --> 00:13:23,380
All right, and the answer of course is no,

363
00:13:23,380 --> 00:13:24,900
because if you're both at the 48th,

364
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:26,900
one person goes to the 49th percentile

365
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:29,340
and then gets at least 51% of the votes.

366
00:13:29,340 --> 00:13:31,900
So we can therefore say that,

367
00:13:31,900 --> 00:13:33,340
well, they must converge to something greater

368
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:34,740
than or equal to the median.

369
00:13:35,700 --> 00:13:37,860
All right, and what is the number

370
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:39,820
that is both greater than or equal to something

371
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:41,940
and less than or equal to something?

372
00:13:41,940 --> 00:13:44,220
Yes, the thing, it is the thing.

373
00:13:44,220 --> 00:13:45,460
The number greater than or equal to seven

374
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:47,820
and less than or equal to seven is seven.

375
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:50,980
All right, so parties converge

376
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:54,900
to the bliss point of the median voter, right?

377
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:57,580
And this is the famous median voter theorem,

378
00:13:57,580 --> 00:14:00,940
which if the parties are named Democrats and Republicans,

379
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:04,700
says the Democratic platform equals the Republican platform

380
00:14:04,700 --> 00:14:06,860
equals the bliss point of the median voter.

381
00:14:08,340 --> 00:14:09,980
Now, there are many implications of this.

382
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:12,980
The most obvious one is if you are trying to understand

383
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:15,380
why policy changes over time,

384
00:14:15,380 --> 00:14:19,060
what is the natural candidate for what has changed?

385
00:14:19,060 --> 00:14:20,020
Median voter.

386
00:14:20,020 --> 00:14:22,820
So why come in 1900 there wasn't any gay marriage?

387
00:14:24,260 --> 00:14:25,580
Median voter didn't want it.

388
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:27,660
And how come now we got it?

389
00:14:27,660 --> 00:14:28,660
Median voter does want it.

390
00:14:28,660 --> 00:14:29,500
That's the simple story.

391
00:14:29,500 --> 00:14:30,460
Now you say, well, that's wrong

392
00:14:30,460 --> 00:14:32,460
because the Supreme Court and everything else.

393
00:14:32,460 --> 00:14:34,860
All right, and that's probably true, but still.

394
00:14:36,620 --> 00:14:37,500
If you were to say, all right, fine.

395
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:40,060
Now, maybe the Supreme Court gave it to the country

396
00:14:40,060 --> 00:14:42,300
10 years before it was going to,

397
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:46,260
but if the Supreme Court in 1900 tried declaring gay marriage

398
00:14:46,260 --> 00:14:48,100
something was gonna happen, right?

399
00:14:48,100 --> 00:14:49,380
Something crazy might happen.

400
00:14:49,380 --> 00:14:52,460
Like they might go and first of all, they'd say, what?

401
00:14:52,460 --> 00:14:54,180
Of course marriage should be gay

402
00:14:54,180 --> 00:14:56,180
because marriages should be happy.

403
00:14:56,180 --> 00:14:57,580
And that's what gay means.

404
00:14:57,580 --> 00:15:00,300
And then you might say, no, no, no, that's not what I mean.

405
00:15:00,300 --> 00:15:01,140
I mean something else.

406
00:15:01,140 --> 00:15:04,220
Like, wait, have you ever explained it

407
00:15:04,220 --> 00:15:05,660
to someone who's in their eighties?

408
00:15:05,660 --> 00:15:07,140
There's just a great deal of confusion

409
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:08,940
about what in the world you're saying.

410
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:09,780
Right?

411
00:15:09,780 --> 00:15:10,980
Men don't marry men.

412
00:15:10,980 --> 00:15:12,780
It's like, well, now they do.

413
00:15:12,780 --> 00:15:13,620
No, they don't.

414
00:15:13,620 --> 00:15:14,820
All right.

415
00:15:14,820 --> 00:15:16,660
Just, well, the median position has changed

416
00:15:16,660 --> 00:15:17,700
and so now they do.

417
00:15:17,700 --> 00:15:18,900
It's like, all right.

418
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:20,180
So that's the idea.

419
00:15:20,180 --> 00:15:21,020
Or if you're saying like,

420
00:15:21,020 --> 00:15:23,660
why do they have socialized medicine in Britain

421
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:25,740
but not in the United States?

422
00:15:25,740 --> 00:15:28,940
The voter in Britain wants socialized medicine.

423
00:15:28,940 --> 00:15:31,380
The median voter in the United States does not want it.

424
00:15:31,380 --> 00:15:34,500
Why is it that immigration restrictions exist

425
00:15:34,500 --> 00:15:36,180
in the United States?

426
00:15:36,180 --> 00:15:38,140
Yes, the median voter, who believe that is the person

427
00:15:38,140 --> 00:15:40,180
who votes in the United States wants it.

428
00:15:40,180 --> 00:15:41,740
You have to be very careful in specifying,

429
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:44,300
well, there's lots of voters in India who don't like it.

430
00:15:44,300 --> 00:15:46,580
But the voters in India don't vote in American elections.

431
00:15:46,580 --> 00:15:49,300
And so their view does not count

432
00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:52,020
and is not what politicians here are competing for.

433
00:15:53,660 --> 00:15:58,180
Now, we don't have a lot of time to go over the empirics

434
00:15:58,180 --> 00:16:01,980
but it is fair to say that this is an extremely

435
00:16:01,980 --> 00:16:03,620
informative model in the same way the supply

436
00:16:03,620 --> 00:16:06,340
and demand model is extremely informative about markets.

437
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:08,740
You know, does supply and demand explain everything?

438
00:16:08,740 --> 00:16:09,580
No.

439
00:16:09,580 --> 00:16:12,380
But on the other hand, if you know supply and demand model,

440
00:16:12,380 --> 00:16:14,100
you can explain a lot more about the world

441
00:16:14,100 --> 00:16:15,380
than other people can.

442
00:16:15,380 --> 00:16:18,940
And furthermore, supply and demand model works way better

443
00:16:18,940 --> 00:16:20,860
than it really should when you think about it

444
00:16:20,860 --> 00:16:24,260
because it seems to actually explain lots of things

445
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:26,020
even in industries that aren't atomistic

446
00:16:26,020 --> 00:16:26,980
and there's other issues.

447
00:16:26,980 --> 00:16:28,740
So it doesn't exactly fit the model

448
00:16:28,740 --> 00:16:30,860
but it's still very predictably accurate.

449
00:16:30,860 --> 00:16:32,940
And I will just foreshadow and say,

450
00:16:32,940 --> 00:16:34,220
when you go into the data,

451
00:16:34,220 --> 00:16:36,620
the median voter model does way better

452
00:16:36,620 --> 00:16:38,260
than you would ever expect.

453
00:16:38,260 --> 00:16:41,260
It's one of these uncannily predictive models.

454
00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:43,580
Which again, the main problem people have is,

455
00:16:43,580 --> 00:16:46,620
well, surely America don't want farm subsidies

456
00:16:46,620 --> 00:16:47,820
and so the model's wrong.

457
00:16:47,820 --> 00:16:49,940
It's like, hmm, what makes you so sure?

458
00:16:49,940 --> 00:16:51,780
Well, it's not in people's interests.

459
00:16:51,780 --> 00:16:54,540
What makes you so sure people vote for their interests?

460
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:58,300
I guess, I just kind of figured they would.

461
00:16:58,300 --> 00:17:00,100
Well, why don't we go and look at the data

462
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:02,540
and see what Americans think about farm subsidies

463
00:17:02,540 --> 00:17:04,980
and then you will go, wow, they're super popular.

464
00:17:04,980 --> 00:17:06,780
The model actually works.

465
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:07,700
I guess.

466
00:17:07,700 --> 00:17:10,860
Yes, what is this an application of the hotel and game?

467
00:17:10,860 --> 00:17:11,980
Of the, which game?

468
00:17:11,980 --> 00:17:13,220
Hotel and game.

469
00:17:13,220 --> 00:17:14,060
Hotel and game.

470
00:17:14,060 --> 00:17:14,900
Hotel and game?

471
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:15,740
Yeah.

472
00:17:15,740 --> 00:17:17,100
Yes, so it's similar to the hotel and game

473
00:17:17,100 --> 00:17:18,900
except in the hotel and game,

474
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:21,660
normally firms are actually not maximizing profits

475
00:17:21,660 --> 00:17:23,420
and so they don't wanna do full convergence

476
00:17:23,420 --> 00:17:24,740
in the hotel and game.

477
00:17:24,740 --> 00:17:25,620
Because in the hotel and game,

478
00:17:25,620 --> 00:17:27,020
if you have full convergence,

479
00:17:27,020 --> 00:17:29,420
then you actually earn no profits.

480
00:17:29,420 --> 00:17:30,940
Whereas this is one where you don't worry,

481
00:17:30,940 --> 00:17:33,180
what you want is to win, right?

482
00:17:33,180 --> 00:17:35,060
So in the hotel and game,

483
00:17:35,060 --> 00:17:37,460
it's you aren't looking to have the larger share of profits,

484
00:17:37,460 --> 00:17:40,060
you're looking to just get to maximize profits

485
00:17:40,060 --> 00:17:42,580
and you don't care if you get more profits

486
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:44,380
but it's a smaller share, it's not a problem.

487
00:17:44,380 --> 00:17:47,660
So the hotel and game has diversions to some degree.

488
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:50,020
But yes, it's a very similar structure.

489
00:17:50,020 --> 00:17:50,860
Yes.

490
00:17:50,860 --> 00:17:53,780
If I'm understanding the median voter theorem correctly,

491
00:17:53,780 --> 00:17:56,460
wouldn't it suggest that most elections

492
00:17:56,460 --> 00:18:00,620
would be 50, 50, 51, 49%?

493
00:18:00,620 --> 00:18:02,740
So that is correct, yes.

494
00:18:02,740 --> 00:18:04,300
And then of course, when applying the models

495
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:06,420
to the real world, you could have the really high threshold

496
00:18:06,420 --> 00:18:08,860
and say, if the election isn't 50, 50 exactly,

497
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:09,820
the model's wrong.

498
00:18:10,700 --> 00:18:13,580
Which is what a stickler could say, certainly.

499
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:14,780
On the other hand, you could go

500
00:18:14,780 --> 00:18:16,620
and just look at the distribution

501
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:18,820
of what the vote shares are

502
00:18:18,820 --> 00:18:21,260
and they are pretty tightly clustered around 50%,

503
00:18:21,260 --> 00:18:23,500
at least for two-party elections normally.

504
00:18:24,700 --> 00:18:27,380
So if you're looking around for when does this not work?

505
00:18:27,380 --> 00:18:29,740
So once in a while, you'll get a party

506
00:18:29,740 --> 00:18:32,580
that gets taken over by an extremist faction

507
00:18:32,580 --> 00:18:33,820
that seems to care more about

508
00:18:33,820 --> 00:18:36,460
having a philosophically correct platform than winning,

509
00:18:36,460 --> 00:18:39,580
such as the Labour Party in England now,

510
00:18:39,580 --> 00:18:40,780
or the Labour Party in England

511
00:18:40,780 --> 00:18:43,540
in the 1970s through the 1980s.

512
00:18:43,540 --> 00:18:44,860
And then you can have a period

513
00:18:44,860 --> 00:18:46,700
where one party is crushing them all the time.

514
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:49,740
So if one party seems like they just stopped trying

515
00:18:49,740 --> 00:18:51,020
to appeal to the median voter,

516
00:18:51,020 --> 00:18:53,060
then you can get this kind of divergence.

517
00:18:53,060 --> 00:18:54,180
The other main case is when you look

518
00:18:54,180 --> 00:18:55,820
at very local government,

519
00:18:55,820 --> 00:18:57,380
then you'll often get, again,

520
00:18:57,380 --> 00:18:58,980
so especially like urban government,

521
00:18:58,980 --> 00:19:01,460
very common to get these very lopsided percentages.

522
00:19:01,460 --> 00:19:05,220
So like 70% or 80% for the Democratic candidate

523
00:19:05,220 --> 00:19:07,940
in a city like New York or San Francisco,

524
00:19:07,940 --> 00:19:12,940
or sometimes the flip side for suburban mayors

525
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:15,820
in very conservative places.

526
00:19:15,820 --> 00:19:18,820
So that's a more interesting counter example.

527
00:19:18,820 --> 00:19:20,940
So that's one where it really looks like

528
00:19:20,940 --> 00:19:23,860
people actually have an aversion to the party.

529
00:19:23,860 --> 00:19:25,860
It's not just the platforms,

530
00:19:25,860 --> 00:19:28,380
but the party themselves is disgusting to them.

531
00:19:28,380 --> 00:19:30,420
Although this does correctly predict, for example,

532
00:19:30,420 --> 00:19:33,260
that what is the Republican Party of Massachusetts like?

533
00:19:33,260 --> 00:19:35,620
It's a very liberal party.

534
00:19:35,620 --> 00:19:37,940
What is the Democratic Party of Texas like?

535
00:19:37,940 --> 00:19:38,780
They're very conservative.

536
00:19:38,780 --> 00:19:43,620
